## The Math That Changes Everything

Here's a question most people never ask themselves: "What if I could buy a property and have my tenants pay for my entire mortgage?"

It sounds too good to be true. But it's not. It's called house hacking, and it's one of the most accessible paths into real estate investing.

The math is simple:
• Buy a duplex or multi-unit property with just 3.5% down (FHA loan)
• Live in one unit
• Rent out the other units
• Tenants pay your mortgage
• You live essentially for free while building wealth

## How House Hacking Works: The $300,000 Example

Let's use real numbers:

The Purchase:
• Duplex purchase price: $300,000
• FHA down payment (3.5%): $10,500
• Your out-of-pocket cost: $10,500

The Monthly Income:
• Unit 1 (where you live): Your cost is your share of the mortgage
• Unit 2 (tenant-occupied): $1,500/month in rent

The Monthly Payment:
• Total mortgage (PITI): $2,000/month
• Your share: $1,000/month (roughly)
• Tenant pays: $1,500/month

The Result:
• You live in your unit for approximately $1,000/month
• The tenant's rent ($1,500) more than covers the mortgage
• You're essentially building equity for free

## Why This Strategy Works

### 1. Reduced Living Expenses

Housing is typically your biggest monthly cost. If you can cut that expense to near-zero while building equity, everything changes.

Instead of:
• Renting for $1,200/month (and building zero equity)

You're:
• Living in a property you own (and building $500+ in equity per month)

Over 10 years, that's $60,000+ in equity from living costs you would have paid anyway.

### 2. Lower Entry Barrier to Real Estate

Traditional real estate investing requires 20-25% down payments. That's $60,000-$75,000 on a $300,000 property.

House hacking with FHA requires 3.5% down. That's $10,500.

FHA financing removes the biggest barrier to entry for new investors.

### 3. Building Equity Using Other People's Money

This is the wealth multiplier moment:

Your tenant pays your mortgage. That mortgage payment includes principal. That principal becomes your equity.

Over 30 years, your tenant essentially pays down your loan while you:
• Live in the property
• Collect appreciation
• Build ownership

This is leverage working for you.

### 4. Perfect Training Ground for Landlording

Most new investors don't know how to manage tenants, handle maintenance issues, or balance P&L.

House hacking gives you real-world experience without the overhead. You live there. You see issues firsthand. You learn the business while still getting paid to stay.

## The Three Levels of House Hacking

### Level 1: Duplex (2 Units)

Example: $300,000 duplex
• Down payment: $10,500
• Monthly rent from tenant: $1,500
• Your monthly housing cost: ~$500
• Annual savings: $6,000+

Entry level. Simple. Proven.

### Level 2: Triplex (3 Units)

Example: $400,000 triplex
• Down payment: $14,000
• Total rental income: $3,000/month (two tenant units)
• Your monthly housing cost: ~$200
• Annual savings: $14,400+

More complex but higher returns. Requires self-sufficiency test with FHA (property must generate enough income to cover expenses).

### Level 3: Fourplex (4 Units)

Example: $480,000 fourplex
• Down payment: $16,800
• Total rental income: $4,200/month (three tenant units)
• Your monthly housing cost: ~-$100 (you actually make money each month)
• Annual income: $1,200+

Now you're profiting. The property generates positive cash flow while you live there.

